qcomtee-qseecom
================

This is a diagnostic userspace client for the QSEECom compatibility service
provided by modern Qualcomm TEE firmware. On recent SoCs, including SM8750,
QSEECom_start_app() is implemented on top of the SMCInvoke object protocol
instead of the legacy /dev/qseecom ABI.

The tool uses the generic Linux TEE object-reference ABI exposed by qcomtee:

  root -> registerLegacy -> ClientEnv -> open(UID 122)
       -> QSEEComCompatAppLoader -> lookupTA/loadFromBuffer

Build it natively or for the target:

  make -C qcomtee
  make -C qcomtee CROSS_COMPILE=aarch64-linux-gnu-

Examples:

  qcomtee-qseecom self-test-hmac
  qcomtee-qseecom probe
  qcomtee-qseecom probe-keymaster-service-version
  qcomtee-qseecom probe-keymaster-service-init
  qcomtee-qseecom probe-keymaster-service-keymint-version
  qcomtee-qseecom probe-keymaster-service-date-support
  qcomtee-qseecom probe-keymaster-service-configure
  qcomtee-qseecom probe-keymaster-service-shared-hmac
  qcomtee-qseecom probe-keymaster-service-shared-hmac-private
  qcomtee-qseecom probe-keymaster-service-shared-hmac-direct-private \
          keymaster64 /root/keymaster64-current-slot-reconstructed.elf
  qcomtee-qseecom lookup fpapp
  qcomtee-qseecom load fpapp /lib/firmware/qcom/xiaomi/piano --hold
  qcomtee-qseecom send fpapp - request.bin response.bin 4096
  qcomtee-qseecom send-modified gfpia - request.bin payload.bin \
          response.bin payload-out.bin 64 request-out.bin
  xiaomi-fp-run --pass-fp-fd qcomtee-qseecom \
          send-gfpia-1000-1001 gfpia /boot/image \
          request-1000.bin payload-1000.bin response-1000.bin \
          payload-out-1000.bin request-out-1000.bin \
          request-1001.bin payload-1001.bin response-1001.bin \
          payload-out-1001.bin request-out-1001.bin [--hlos-init]
  xiaomi-fp-run --pass-fp-fd qcomtee-qseecom \
          send-gfpia-1000-1001-1005 gfpia /boot/image \
          request-1000.bin payload-1000.bin response-1000.bin \
          payload-out-1000.bin request-out-1000.bin \
          request-1001.bin payload-1001.bin response-1001.bin \
          payload-out-1001.bin request-out-1001.bin \
          request-1005.bin payload-1005.bin response-1005.bin \
          payload-out-1005.bin request-out-1005.bin
  goodix-gfpia-gen.py piano-init \
          --cap-s-model piano-goodix-cap-s-2.bin \
          --mistouch-model piano-goodix-mistouch.bin \
          --output-dir piano-init-input
  mkdir piano-init-output
  xiaomi-fp-run --pass-fp-fd qcomtee-qseecom \
          send-gfpia-piano-init gfpia /boot/image \
          piano-init-input piano-init-output
  goodix-gfpia-gen.py piano-active-group \
          --cap-s-model piano-goodix-cap-s-2.bin \
          --mistouch-model piano-goodix-mistouch.bin \
          --output-dir piano-active-group-input
  mkdir piano-active-group-output
  xiaomi-fp-run --pass-fp-fd qcomtee-qseecom \
          send-gfpia-piano-active-group gfpia /boot/image \
          piano-active-group-input piano-active-group-output
  mkdir piano-hmac-output
	  xiaomi-fp-run --pass-fp-fd qcomtee-qseecom \
	          send-gfpia-piano-hmac gfpia /boot/image \
	          piano-active-group-input piano-hmac-output \
	          /root/keymaster64-slot-b-reconstructed.elf

For load, SOURCE can be a reconstructed ELF image or a directory containing
APP.b00, APP.b01, and so on. Split images are reconstructed using program
header offsets in APP.b00, matching Qualcomm's SM8750 securemsm driver. The
kernel transport limits a single invocation to a 4 MiB inbound buffer. Images
that fit use loadFromBuffer; larger images, including piano's gfpia, are placed
in QTEE shared memory and loaded with loadFromRegion.

The credentials used for registerLegacy match Qualcomm's in-kernel QSEECom
compatibility client (UID 1000, package UefiSmcInvoke). Secure firmware still
enforces its own service and TA policy. A successful lookup/load only proves
that the compatibility object and TA are reachable; it does not establish the
fingerprint command protocol.

`probe-keymaster-service-version` follows the KeyMint service's object path:
ClientEnv UID 151 returns an IAppClient,
`IAppClient.getAppObject("keymaster64")` returns an IOpener, and
`IOpener.open(150)` returns the IKMHal object. It sends only Keymaster command
`0x200` through IKMHal and prints the non-secret version fields. It neither
uses the QSEECom app loader nor requests HMAC material.

`probe-keymaster-service-init` extends the same path with the fixed
set-version request observed in the public SM8750 KeyMint client. It first
requires a successful `0x200`, then sends the 24-byte request
`[0x207, 4, 5, 4, 5, 0]` on the same IKMHal object. It stops before Keymaster
configure, shared-HMAC commands, and all fingerprint hardware operations.

`probe-keymaster-service-keymint-version` repeats the successful `0x200` and
`0x207` lifecycle, then sends the first non-legacy configure prerequisite from
the public Xiaomi SM8750 Keymaster client. The exact request is command
`0x3121` followed by the five-byte CBOR map `{ 11: 300 }`. It stops before the
`0x2116` configure-parameter request, shared-HMAC commands, GFPiA, and all
fingerprint hardware operations.

`probe-keymaster-service-date-support` repeats get-version and set-version on
one IKMHal object, then sends the 12-byte legacy query used by the public
Xiaomi client: little-endian command `0x215` followed by eight zero bytes. A
zero status selects full `YYYYMMDD` patchlevels; status `-40` selects
`YYYYMM`. It stops before `0x3121`, configure, HMAC, GFPiA, and all fingerprint
hardware operations.

`probe-keymaster-service-configure` repeats the successful `0x200`, `0x207`,
and `0x3121` lifecycle, then sends a fixed Piano `0x2116` CBOR parameter map.
The values come from the preserved Android A slot: OS version `160000`, OS
patchlevel `20260701`, and vendor patchlevel `20260201`. It stops before HMAC,
GFPiA, and all fingerprint hardware operations.

`probe-keymaster-service-shared-hmac` keeps one IKMHal object for the fixed
sequence `0x200`, `0x207`, the 12-byte `0x215` date query, `0x3121`, the
three-tag full-date `0x2116` configure request, `0x220e`, and one `0x220f`.
The date query must return status zero; the month-only `-40` result stops the
sequence before configure or HMAC. Before `0x220e`, the process disables core
dumps and makes itself non-dumpable, failing closed if either operation fails.
The get response must be exact canonical CBOR with 32-byte seed and nonce
values. The compute request returns that pair immediately as one standard
64-byte record, and its response must contain an exact 32-byte sharing check.
All response and secret-bearing buffers are cleared on every path and no
contents are printed or persisted. This bounded probe does not try the
72-byte extension, retry `0x220f`, invoke private `0x205`, load GFPiA, or open
the fingerprint device.

`probe-keymaster-service-shared-hmac-private` is the only service CLI that
sends the private request `{ command = 0x205, selector = 2 }`. It keeps one
IKMHal object for the complete sequence `0x200`, `0x207`, the full-date
`0x215` gate, `0x3121`, the three-tag `0x2116` configure request, `0x220e`,
one `0x220f`, and exactly one `0x205`. It inherits the fail-closed core-dump
and process-dump hardening applied before `0x220e`. Any failure stops the
sequence and the private command is never retried.

IKMHal can report its complete output capacity for the private command. A
zero-status result must therefore contain at least 0x100 bytes, but validation
uses only a borrowed fixed 0x100-byte logical view. Encapsulated-message bounds
must fit that view and cannot extend into the transport tail. The complete
owning service output, private request, and every shared-HMAC temporary are
cleared on all paths. No secret bytes are printed or persisted. This isolated
probe stops after validating the private response; it does not load GFPiA,
issue command 109, use the QSEECom app loader, or open the fingerprint device.
The older incomplete service sequence `0x200 -> 0x207 -> 0x205` is not exposed
as a CLI.

`probe-keymaster-service-shared-hmac-direct-private` is a separate direct
fallback and requires the fixed app name `keymaster64` plus an explicit
`SOURCE`. The source must be a reconstructed ELF or split-image directory from
the device's current slot. The client validates that source before opening the
Keymaster service, immediately clears its validation owner, then completes
`0x200 -> 0x207 -> 0x215 -> 0x3121 -> 0x2116 -> 0x220e -> 0x220f` on one
IKMHal object. Only after every public gate succeeds does it open the UID 122
QSEECom app loader and obtain a direct controller with the normal
lookup-or-load behavior. The log states whether the TA was already available
through lookup or was loaded from the validated source.

The fallback never sends service command `0x205`. It sends the direct request
`{ command = 0x205, selector = 2 }` exactly once, requires the returned request
to remain the same eight bytes, and requires an exact 0x100-byte response with
message bounds wholly inside that fixed envelope. The direct controller is
released, and the request, returned request, and complete response owner are
cleared on every path. It stops without a retry, GFPiA, command 109, the
fingerprint device, or any response export. `SOURCE` is structurally checked,
but the client cannot determine the active Android slot; selecting the actual
current-slot image remains an operator requirement.

The send-modified command covers the one-buffer ABI used by piano's Goodix
fingerprint client: it allocates QTEE shared memory for PAYLOAD, passes it as
the first memory object, and asks QSEEComCompat to patch its address at offset
0 in REQUEST. The four-entry embedded-offset array is retained to match the
legacy modified-command ABI; the other three memory objects are NULL. RESPONSE
and the possibly modified payload are written to separate output files.
Supplying the optional REQUEST_OUT argument after RESPONSE_SIZE also saves the
request bytes returned by QSEEComCompat, so the memory-object patch can be
verified without putting a physical address in the input request.

Do not use the plain send command with gfpia. Every observed gfpia business
request uses QSEECom_send_modified_cmd; omitting the memory object leaves a
zero secure-side payload address and can reboot the device. The tool rejects
that combination. For gfpia, send-modified also requires a 0x80-byte request,
a 0x40-byte response, a zero 64-bit patch field at request offset 0, and a
payload file whose size matches the uint32 value at request offset 0x24. The
first observed command uses a 0x280-byte payload, but send-modified
intentionally does not synthesize or issue that command. Inspect and validate
the complete request before invoking an unknown fingerprint operation.

The fixed send-gfpia-1000-1001 command is narrower than send-modified. It only
accepts the reviewed cold-start inputs: command 1000 at probe level 4 with
token/sequence 1/0, followed by command 1001 with token/sequence 2/1 and no
cached OTP. It keeps one QSEECom controller and requires an inherited
descriptor for the sensor's UIO device from xiaomi-fp-run, which is what proves
the sensor is powered while the sequence runs. The byte command 1001 carries is
a constant: the driver that used to be asked for it by ioctl answered zero
unconditionally. Each command's
patched request, response, and returned payload are synchronized to disk before
their status is checked. Any nonzero response status, address outside the low
32-bit range, or unexpected command-1000 configuration stops the sequence.
Without a final option, the command stops after validating command 1001, as it
did before HLOS initialization support was added.

The separate `send-gfpia-1000-1001-1005` command implements the reviewed
zero-model diagnostic path. It additionally requires command 1005 with
token/sequence 3/2 and a 0x74-byte payload whose only nonzero input is the
sequence field. It always performs HLOS initialization: there is no option to
send command 1005 before `INIT`, `ENABLE_IRQ`, and `CHIP_INFO`. The IRQ remains
enabled across command 1005, then is disabled on every exit. All three
commands use the same QSEECom controller, and all nine output files are opened
before the first secure request so a bad output path cannot interrupt the
hardware sequence halfway through. Stock permits zero configured model files,
so this path deliberately sends no command 1113.

Piano's captured command-1000 configuration is not a zero-model
configuration. It enables both the CAP_S_2 ASP model and the Piano palm
mistouch model. `send-gfpia-piano-init` implements that device-specific path:
1000 token/sequence 1/0, 1001 2/1, three ASP transport chunks 3/2 through
5/4, three palm chunks 6/5 through 8/7, and 1005 9/8. The input directory
must be created by `goodix-gfpia-gen.py piano-init`; the generator verifies
both embedded stock-model SHA-256 values and writes a `SHA256SUMS` manifest.
The output directory must already exist. Before the first secure request the
client validates all nine request/payload pairs, reconstructs and checks both
logical model layouts, allocates all shared buffers, and opens all 27 output
files with `O_EXCL`. It also requires command 1000 to report Piano chip 0x31,
palm enabled, ASP enabled, and CAP_S rather than CAP_T. Each returned model
chunk is synchronized and checked before the next chunk. Unlike the stock HAL,
the tool stops at the first model error rather than swallowing it and
continuing to command 1005.

`send-gfpia-piano-active-group` is a separate, narrower extension of that
known-good sequence. It adds class-1 command 1014 at token/sequence 10/9 on
the same controller, fixes gid to 0, and fixes the only accepted pathname to
`/data/vendor_de/0/fpdata`. The generator zero-fills the entire 0x270-byte
payload, places the 24 pathname bytes at offset 0x70, and leaves a NUL at
0x88. The client validates all ten pairs and opens all 30 output files before
command 1000. Command 1014 can migrate or synchronize fingerprint state via
the filesystem listeners; a zero TA status does not prove every optional file
operation succeeded, so check the supplicant log and state directory as well.
This command does not fetch or install the Keymaster HMAC key.

`send-gfpia-piano-hmac` extends that exact active-group sequence. After the
nine initialization requests and command 1014 have succeeded, it sends the
plain Keymaster request `{ command = 0x205, selector = 2 }`, validates the
fixed 0x100-byte response, releases the `keymaster64` controller, and passes
the complete response unchanged to GFPiA class-2 command 109 with token 11.
The inter-app message is not a normal GFPiA common-prefix payload, so the
client never writes a sequence at offset 0x44. `KEYMASTER_SOURCE` must point
to a `keymaster64` ELF reconstructed on the device from that device's current
slot `keymaster` partition; do not substitute an image from the other slot.
The Keymaster response and all command-109 HMAC buffers remain in memory:
their bytes are never printed or written to an output file and are explicitly
cleared after use, including error cleanup. Only non-secret status information
is printed.

The HMAC request builders, response bounds, fixed command-109 fields, and
sensitive-buffer clearing can be checked without a QTEE device:

  qcomtee-qseecom self-test-hmac

The final `--hlos-init` option and both fixed completion sequences are
explicit hardware-driving extensions. HLOS initialization occurs only after all
command-1001 outputs have been synchronized to disk and the normal result
validation has succeeded. An additional gate requires an
exact 0x168-byte payload, preserved `fw_info`, match version 2, and an OTP
length no larger than 0xc0. The client then issues `INIT` on the same inherited
fingerprint fd and requires netlink route 25, followed by `ENABLE_IRQ` and an
eight-byte, zero-initialized `CHIP_INFO`. The chip vendor byte comes from
command-1001 payload offset 0x88; mode and operation are the low bytes of 1000
and 52. Once `ENABLE_IRQ` succeeds, the client attempts `DISABLE_IRQ` on every
subsequent exit before releasing the QSEECom controller. In the 1005 sequence,
the TA may return orientation and navigation polarity at payload offsets
0x6c/0x6e; both enter the TA as zero and are not caller configuration. Do not
use either hardware-driving path unless the required TrustZone listeners are
already serving requests; `INIT` can block in secure-world callbacks and
actively accesses the sensor.

qcomtee-fs-supplicant
=====================

This target-side filesystem supplicant registers Qualcomm listeners 10 and
0x7000 for TrustZone applications that use the legacy QSEECom filesystem
protocols. It also supplies the credentials callback needed to open the
listener registration service. Four callback workers share one /dev/tee0
context so a TrustZone request can wait on another listener without deadlocking
the userspace service.

Run exactly one instance before loading gfpia:

  qcomtee-fs-supplicant --state-root /var/lib/xiaomi-fingerprint

Absolute Android paths are stored below the state root. For example,
/data/vendor/fpdump/gf_otp_info.so maps to
/var/lib/xiaomi-fingerprint/data/vendor/fpdump/gf_otp_info.so. Path fields
follow Qualcomm's C-string ABI: bytes after the first NUL are ignored because
TrustZone may reuse a request buffer for a shorter path. The supplicant still
rejects unterminated paths, parent traversal, symlinked path components, and
non-regular GPFS objects. File handles returned to TrustZone are opaque values
rather than host file descriptors. Offset-zero GPFS writes use a same-directory
temporary file, synchronize it, preserve the previous file as .bak, and
atomically replace the destination.

The built-in protocol and path-safety tests do not access /dev/tee0:

  qcomtee-fs-supplicant --self-test --state-root /tmp/qcomtee-fs-test

The supplicant only services callbacks. It does not synthesize fingerprint
calibration data and does not touch the sensor.

xiaomi-fp-run
=============

This target-side diagnostic wrapper holds the sensor's UIO device open while
running a child command, which is the whole of keeping the sensor powered: the
driver raises the supply, pulses reset and unmasks the interrupt on open, and
undoes all of it on close. The wrapper finds the device by the name in
/sys/class/uio/uioN/name rather than by a fixed minor, and closes it after the
child exits or when it receives SIGINT, SIGTERM, or SIGHUP.

With --pass-fp-fd, the wrapper passes a duplicate of that open file to the
child in XIAOMI_FP_FD, so the child can read interrupts from the same
descriptor rather than opening the device a second time and racing the
supply.

For example, a separately reviewed gfpia request can be sent while the HLOS
signals remain configured:

  xiaomi-fp-run qcomtee-qseecom send-modified gfpia /boot/image \
          request.bin payload.bin response.bin payload-out.bin 64

The wrapper does not create TA requests and does not interpret their results.
